3. LinuxMachines
---

Some basic Linux know how is required as well as some machine hardware knowledge to use this cluster.

Knowledge of and potentially some understanding of these linux commands/concepts are required to use the cluster effectively:

* `ls`
  Examples:
  ```
  ls    # lists files in current directory
  ls -a    # lists all files includes ones starting with "."
  ls foo   # lists files in directory called foo
  ```

* `cd`
  Examples:
  ```
  cd ~    # change directory of terminal to home
  cd foo   # change directory of terminal to foo folder
  ```

* `mkdir`
  Examples:
  ```
  mkdir foo    # make directory called "foo"
  ```

* `cat`
  Examples:
  ```
  cat foo.txt    # print contents of file
  ```

* `rm`
  Examples:
  ```
  rm foo.txt    # delete file
  rm -r foo    # delete directory
  ```

* `chmod`
  Examples:
  ```
  chmod +x script    # makes script executable
  ```

* `man`
  Examples:
  ```
  man ls    # show manual page about command
  man mkdir
  man cat
  ```
  Note: `cd` doesn't have a manual page, to view more information try `help cd`

* SSH - you can think of SSH as logging into a machine and gaining access to a terminal. Any command you type after SSHing into a machine is you running that command on said machine, *not* your own machine. You will be SSHing into the head node so you'll need to know about the command `ssh <dide-username>@wpia-lxhn.hpc.dide.ic.ac.uk` and (optional) how you can make it easier for yourself with an SSH config file

* Some understanding of environment variables in bash - all env vars are captured by Slurm so any env vars you set in your terminal will propagate when your job is run, e.g. $PATH, $PWD and $HOME


Understanding of these machine concepts are required to use the cluster:

* Difference between cores, threads and processes - this is a decent article that explains the difference: "https://thecodinggopher.substack.com/p/processes-vs-threads-vs-cores". You don't need to know all the detail in there but the main takeaways are processes contain threads and a core can multitask between multiple threads. In particular, this means when requesting cores from Slurm, the number of threads and processes are *not* the number of cores required for the job.

* Difference between memory/RAM and storage space - memory/RAM is temporary space a program needs to run, it depends on how much temporary data is required by the program. Storage space is how much permanent space you need to save files to your home directory. When requesting memory from Slurm, make sure to not confuse it with the total size of the files you'll be saving from your job.

* Monitoring GPU usage via `nvidia-smi` - knowledge of `nvidia-smi` command is required as this is a GPU cluster, here is a cheatsheet: "https://gist.github.com/omerfsen/8ecb620675525ac724a92bdf5a31a4b3". It is good etiquette to make sure you are using the resources you have requested rather than wasting them. Before you run a GPU job, please try a smaller job and run `nvidia-smi` on the node that has picked the job up. So, if gpu2 picks up your job, you can run `srun --nodelist=gpu2 nvidia-smi` and make sure you have a high enough power draw, memory usage and GPU utilisation.

(!) it can be hard to know which GPUs on the machine your task has been allocated. In your sbatch script you can add "echo $CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES" which prints out the indices of GPUs that have been allocated to your job. Do *not* modify this env var.

There are plenty more resources online to help you understand all of these concepts better.
